Most Common Git Commands (1–2 Line Interview Answers)
Command	Answer
git init	Initializes a new Git repository in the current directory.
git clone <url>	Creates a local copy of a remote repository.
git status	Shows the current state of working directory and staging area.
git add <file>	Adds file changes to the staging area for the next commit.
git add .	Stages all modified and new files in the current directory.
git commit -m "msg"	Saves staged changes to the local repository with a message.
git log	Displays commit history of the repository.
git log --oneline	Shows commit history in a compact one-line format.
git diff	Displays unstaged changes between working directory and repository.
git diff --staged	Shows differences between staged files and last commit.
git branch	Lists all local branches and highlights the current branch.
git branch <name>	Creates a new branch.
git checkout <branch>	Switches to another branch.
git checkout -b <branch>	Creates and switches to a new branch in one command.
git switch <branch>	Modern command to switch branches.
git switch -c <branch>	Creates and switches to a new branch.
git merge <branch>	Combines changes from another branch into the current branch.
git rebase <branch>	Reapplies commits on top of another branch for a cleaner history.
git fetch	Downloads latest changes from remote without merging.
git pull	Fetches and merges changes from the remote repository.
git push	Uploads local commits to the remote repository.
git remote -v	Displays configured remote repositories.
git remote add origin <url>	Adds a remote repository named origin.
git stash	Temporarily saves uncommitted changes.
git stash pop	Restores the most recently stashed changes.
git stash list	Lists all stashed changes.
git reset --soft HEAD~1	Removes the last commit but keeps changes staged.
git reset --mixed HEAD~1	Removes the last commit and unstages changes.
git reset --hard HEAD~1	Removes the last commit and discards all changes.
git revert <commit-id>	Creates a new commit that reverses a previous commit.
git rm <file>	Deletes a file and stages the deletion.
git mv old new	Renames or moves a file and stages the change.
git tag <tag-name>	Creates a tag for a specific commit, usually for releases.
git cherry-pick <commit-id>	Applies a specific commit from another branch.
git show <commit-id>	Displays detailed information about a commit.
git blame <file>	Shows who last modified each line in a file.
git clean -fd	Removes untracked files and directories.
git restore <file>	Discards local changes in a file.
git restore --staged <file>	Removes a file from the staging area.
git config --global user.name "name"	Sets the global Git username.
git config --global user.email "email"	Sets the global Git email address.
Interview Quick Summary
git fetch → Download changes only.
git pull → Download + merge changes.
git merge → Combine branch histories.
git rebase → Move commits to create linear history.
git reset → Remove commits/history locally.
git revert → Safely undo a commit by creating a new commit.
git stash → Temporarily save work without committing.
git cherry-pick → Copy a specific commit from another branch.
